# Divider-directed translation with a trailing stripe

Use when a same-color mask lies beside a uniform full-height divider, and demos appear to move the mask toward the divider while introducing a stripe on the opposite side.

## Coordinate audit

For each demo, record:

- divider column and color;
- source mask's complete occupied coordinate set and bounding box (include disconnected cells in the mask if they form part of the same designed pattern);
- target mask's complete occupied coordinate set;
- common displacement vector, checked on every occupied source cell;
- introduced stripe's column, color, height, and relation to the translated mask's bounding box;
- all unchanged cells, especially source-pattern holes and background gaps.

A useful candidate rule is rigid translation of the exact mask toward the divider until the translated bounding box is adjacent to it. The new full-height stripe may lie one cell beyond the translated mask, away from the divider, but treat that as a hypothesis and confirm it in every pair. Do not fill internal holes or move components independently unless demonstrated.

## Validation

Mechanically transform every demo input and require exact equality with its output, including row/column dimensions, stripe extent, preserved divider, and untouched background. Then apply to the test with bounds/collision checks and count the complete delta set. In the reviewed examples, the divider-to-mask orientation is important: a source shape at columns 2–4 became adjacent to divider at column 7, while another at columns 10–15 became adjacent to divider at column 8; the output added a stripe beyond the translated shape's far edge. These illustrate a candidate alignment, not a substitute for checking the exact demo deltas in a new instance.
